

🧬 1. LawGenome (expanded)

The genome must now support:

scalar parameters

exponents

operator weights

regime thresholds

mixer biases

nonlocal kernels

warp curvature couplings

Here’s the expanded structure:

GenomeParameters — numeric values

GenomeOperators — which operators exist

GenomeMixerBias — how weights shift

GenomeRegimeRules — thresholds for switching physics

This gives the genome the ability to reshape the entire physics stack.

🔧 2. Mutation Operators (new)

We now define three classes of mutation:

MicroMutations — tiny parameter shifts

MacroMutations — exponent changes, operator swaps

StructuralMutations — adding/removing operators, altering mixer logic

This is where the engine becomes alive.

Examples:

warp exponent (p) shifts by ±0.05

nonlocal kernel radius changes

chaotic operator strength toggles

mixer weight function changes shape

a new operator is added to the meta‑equation

This is evolution of laws, not states.

🏆 3. Selection Engine (new)

The selection engine evaluates universes using fitness functions that you define.

Categories:

StabilityFitness

SymmetryFitness

WarpCoherenceFitness

NonlocalBalanceFitness

PatternFormationFitness

Each universe gets a score.The top fraction survives.The rest are replaced by mutated offspring.

This is Darwin applied to physics.

🌌 4. Population Manager (new)

This is the layer that:

spawns universes

runs them

evaluates them

selects survivors

mutates offspring

repeats

It becomes a meta‑simulation running above your physics engine.

The Population Manager uses:

TournamentSelection

Elitism

DiversityPreservation

This prevents the system from collapsing into one boring law set.

